+---
+id: task-003-give-back-single-use-coupon-on-cancel-check-free-delivery-at-50-euros
+type: task
+title: "Give back single-use coupon on cancel; check free delivery at 50 euros"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Two reports about the order service:
+
+1. **Welcome code refused after a same-day cancellation (customer, Paolo).** He placed an order with his
+   one-time welcome code, cancelled it the same day (wrong size), and the corrected order was refused
+   with the code as already used.
+2. **Delivery charged at exactly €50.00 of goods (support team).** A customer paid €4.90 delivery on an
+   order whose goods came to exactly €50.00, while the site promises free delivery from that amount.
+
+## Cause
+
+1. `OrderService.cancel` (`src/orders.ts`) calls `this.#coupons.giveBack(order.coupon, order.customer)`.
+   `Coupons` records a single-use coupon's use by the **order id** (`#usedBy` maps code → order id, see
+   `Coupons.use` in `src/coupons.ts`), and `giveBack` only frees the code when the recorded order is the
+   one given. Passing the customer name means the match never holds, so the code stays used. Reproduced:
+   after placing and cancelling `ORD-0001` with a single-use code, `usedBy` still reads `ORD-0001` and
+   the re-order is refused with "already used". The fix is to pass `order.id`.
+2. Not reproduced as a defect. `shippingCost` (`src/shipping.ts`) ships free when goods are at least
+   `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM_CENTS` (5000). Two books at €25.00 quote €50.00 goods and €0.00 shipping, and
+   `test/orders.test.ts` already pins "free from exactly €50.00". The README says the threshold is
+   measured "after every discount and before VAT". So an order at €50.00 gross that a volume discount or
+   coupon takes below €50.00 correctly pays delivery under the documented rule. The order at issue has
+   not been identified, so its discounts are unknown. See *Open question*.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- A single-use coupon used by an order that is then cancelled is usable again by a new order.
+- A single-use coupon used by an order that is shipped is still refused for the next order (unchanged).
+- Goods of exactly €50.00 after every discount ship free; goods of €25.00 pay delivery. (Already covered
+  by `test/orders.test.ts`; unchanged.)
+- `npm test` passes: the existing 20 tests unchanged, plus a regression test for the cancel case.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- Report 1 fixed: `cancel` passes `order.id` to `giveBack`. Regression test
+  `gives a single-use coupon back when its order is cancelled the same day` in `test/coupons.test.ts`
+  fails without the fix and passes with it. `npm test`: 21 pass, 0 fail.
+- Report 2: no code change. `shippingCost` already ships free from €50.00 of goods after discounts,
+  as the README states. The customer's order is still needed to tell whether a discount applied; see
+  *Open question*.
+
+## Scope
+
+- `src/orders.ts`: `cancel` gives the coupon back for the order's id.
+- `test/orders.test.ts` or `test/coupons.test.ts`: regression test for cancel-then-reuse of a single-use coupon.
+- No change to shipping thresholds, VAT, stock, or the cancellation window.
+
+## Open question (not implemented)
+
+Report 2 needs the customer's order (its order id, or its lines and coupon) to say which rule applied. If
+the order had no discount and its goods were €50.00 at the time of charge, the code is wrong and this
+task must be reopened. If a volume discount or coupon brought the goods below €50.00, the README rule
+applies and delivery was charged correctly; the site's promise wording may then need to say "after
+discounts". Changing the threshold itself is a business rule change and belongs to the product owner.
+
+## Effect
+
+- A single-use coupon is usable again after its order is cancelled, from now on. Orders already
+  cancelled keep their current state; their coupons stay used until a support decision.
